  "rationale": "Evidence from iteration 7 (score 712.2, best so far): the oval (20/20, 1.97 s) and s-bend (20/20, 2.19 s) both finish. The slalom still ends 20/20 off_track, but progress rose from 0.16 to 0.56. That is the largest slalom gain so far, and it came from iteration 7's two changes: a shorter body (0.18 to 0.14 m, lowering yaw inertia about 15 percent), a lighter mass (0.8 to 0.7 kg), and base_speed 1.0 to 0.9. Sensors, weights and gains were unchanged. The slalom gain therefore points to inertia and speed as the limit, not sensing or gain. The max_error of 0.063 m sits at the 0.06 m off-track limit, so the robot is still using all its margin on the swings.\n\nChange: continue the same direction. body_length 0.14 to 0.12 m, mass 0.7 to 0.6 kg, and base_speed 0.9 to 0.85. Yaw inertia falls by about 25 percent from iteration 6 (body_length^2 + body_width^2 goes from 0.0520 to 0.0370 at fixed width). Lower mass also helps acceleration in the bends. The speed cut is small, to keep the slalom from losing the tape on the first swing. Sensors, weights, kp 6.5, kd 2.0, ki 0.2 and line_loss are kept, so the effect is isolated to inertia and speed. body_width stays at 0.12 m because the off-track limit is already at its 0.06 m floor.\n\nExpected result: slalom progress rising above 0.7, with some finishes and fewer line losses (currently 0.25 per run). The oval and s-bend should still finish. The oval time may rise to about 2.1 to 2.2 s, costing a few points on the time term. Risks: a shorter, lighter body may oscillate more on straights, so watch steering_oscillation. If the oval oscillation exceeds 0.4, the next step is kd 1.7. If the slalom still exits at the first swing with line losses, the next step is kp 7.0 with kd 2.2.\n\nHypotheses: H1, yaw inertia still limits the slalom, so further reduction gives more progress. H2, speed 0.85 is near the point where the robot keeps the tape through the back-to-back bends. Measurements wanted next: progress, completion and line_loss_events on the slalom to see whether the gain continues; max_error to see how much margin is left; steering_oscillation on the oval and s-bend for chatter from the lighter, shorter body; completion_time on the oval to measure the speed cost.",
